# tagged release so a published result names the version that produced it.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: Ankora
message: >-
  If you use Ankora in work you publish, please cite it using this metadata,
  naming the version or commit that produced your results alongside the
  underlying scientific tools executed.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Olascoaga
    given-names: Samael
    email: olaskuaga@gmail.com
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0008-5522-7938'
abstract: >-
  Ankora is a local-first desktop workbench for molecular docking and virtual
  screening on Windows and Linux that keeps the scientist in control of every
  inspection, preparation, execution and review decision. It preserves imported
  structures and compound libraries unchanged, requires each chemical and
  microstate decision to be explicit rather than inferred, and records the
  SHA-256 provenance of every transformation. Docking runs locally against
  AutoDock Vina, AutoDock4 CPU and AutoDock-GPU over immutable AutoGrid4 maps,
  and (on Linux) GNINA 1.3.3 convolutional neural-network scoring, refinement
  and ensembles without pooling incompatible scoring scales. Results, in-place
  crystallographic redocking validation, exact-pose ProLIF interaction analysis
  and reproducible campaign bundles are recorded per project, and Ankora renders
  only the Methods prose a campaign's own verified records support — flagging
  unrecorded details explicitly rather than inventing text.
version: 0.1.0
date-released: '2026-08-31'
license: MIT
